of old working class people getting together and fighting for better toilets or something, I would say, better washing machines. Better washing machines. Well, this is the Samba review of history. Anyway, let's get back to Austin Curry and that house in Caledon. You know, asked about Paris, 1968. Of course, it's also the Prague Spring, student protests in America, all of this kind of thing. And when people read about Austin Curry occupying the house, they're like, yes, this is exciting. You know, the revolution is coming to Northern Ireland. Brilliant. And a few weeks after his Caledan protest, Curry goes to a meeting of the civil rights groups. And he says, let's do something bigger. Let's have a march like they have in America.

