Everybody was bracing themselves. What they believed was going to be a massive attack from their nation. Nationalists on the Falls Road. Of course, the people on the Falls Road are expecting exactly the same thing. They're like, oh, the people on the Shankill Road are about to attack us. So we enter the third day of this, Thursday, the 14th of August. In Derry, the RUC are basically exhausted. They've lost control.

126
00:59:36.000 --> 01:00:12.000
They send out a call, mobilize all the B specials. And all these B specials start to assemble outside the bogside with all their weapons, right? They've got makeshift weapons and all this. They're intermingle with crowds of problems. Protestant teenagers and young men and whatnot, getting ready to launch, like, let's go in and take this by force. In Belfast, things are actually, if anything, even worse. It's properly kicked off in Belfast. So there are loyalist gangs roaming the streets. They're throwing petrol bombs into Catholic schools. They're burning down Catholic houses. You know, we're right back in the 1920s, back in 1921 or 22 or something.

127
01:00:12.000 --> 01:00:48.000
Meanwhile, we've got the first gunfights between the police and the IRA. Remember, the IRA had gone quiet in the 1960s, but they had not. not gone away. And the local IRA commander of Belfast, Liam McMillan, has called up some of his old comrades, says, come on, we've got to go and defend our people. But you asked about guns before. They don't have many guns. They have two submachine guns. They've got a Lee Enfield rifle, and they've got six revolvers. So that's sort of all competing to see who gets the guns. And the fighting becomes concentrated on Divis Street. So when you look at the map, the people are interested, Divis Street is basically the entrance to the Catholic Falls Road area. And there you have

128
01:00:48.000 --> 01:01:34.000
The IRA firing with these two machine guns and whatnot at loyalists and the police and the RUC are firing back with armoured cars with heavy machine guns on the armored cars. And in all of this kind of chaos, the armored cars start firing into the high-rise flats, the Divis Street flats. And they hit a Catholic schoolboy called Patrick Rooney who was in bed trying to sleep through this. And he was the first child killed in the troubles. He was just nine years old. Actually, the amazing thing is that the casualties aren't even higher. Seven people were killed in total. Two of them shot by the IRA, one by loyalists, and four of them by the security forces.

129
01:01:34.000 --> 01:02:11.000
Probably 1,000 injured. It's hard to tell because it was often said that Catholics would be less likely to go to hospital. But what's very ominous, of the people injured, more than 100 of them are injured with gunshot wounds. So this is the point where the shooting has really started in earnest. 200 homes are destroyed, probably get just under 2,000 families have to flee their homes and a lot of those end up basically going as refugees across the Irish border. I mean, as melodramatic as it sounds, the Irish government actually established kind of refugee camps on the southern side of the border.

130
01:02:11.000 --> 01:02:32.000
Now, in Britain, Harold Wilson and Jim Callaghan, who of course can't have been expected. to do anything about this because they don't know anything about it. They're watching the TV pictures in absolute horror because it's pretty clear at this point that unless something changes, Northern Ireland is going to head for a full-scale kind of civil war. And the pressure to intervene is overwhelming. So the civil rights campaigners,

131
01:02:32.000 --> 01:02:56.000
people like John Hume and Jerry Fit, in the last couple of days, have started begging Westminster to send troops. Jerry Fit tells the story that on the 14th of August, he's in Belfast, he's actually taken refuge from the fighting in a bookmakers. That's his excuse anyway. He's in a book is and he says a crowd of Catholic people came in and they were quote screaming and shouting that they were going to be murdered in their

132
01:02:56.000 --> 01:03:37.000
beds and begging, begging me to get the army in. And he rang James Callahan and said, I'm pleading with you, Jim, send in the army and I'll never forget his reply. Jerry, he says, I can get the army in, but it's going to be a devil of a job to get it out. It's a very good story whether this actually happened. I don't know. But actually the decisive request comes from Stormont itself from the Northern Ireland government. Late that afternoon, the 14th of August, James Chichester Clark formally asked London to send troops into the streets of Derry. Now Callaghan, when he got the call, was on a plane. He was coming back, almost unbelievably, he was coming back from the Silly Isles,

133
01:03:37.000 --> 01:04:08.000
where he'd gone to meet Harold Wilson, who was on holiday in his shorts. And he got a note from the navigator on the plane, basically Chichester Clark, was asking you to send in the troops, permission to send in the troops, and Callahan scribbled a note on it and handed it back to the navigator, the words permission granted. And at 5 o'clock that afternoon, the first battalion of the Yorkshire regiment marched towards the bogside. And almost immediately at that point, the various combatants kind of down tools

134
01:04:08.000 --> 01:04:41.000
and retreated back into their homes and whatnot. And the next afternoon, the 15th, Calan got a second request. This time can you send troops into Belfast as well? And by nightfall on the 15th, British soldiers are marching down the Catholic Falls Road. And this is a massive moment in this story. A massive moment. I mean, we grew up with British troops on the streets of Northern Ireland, but this is when it all started. And all those kind of search points and helicopters and things.

135
01:04:41.000 --> 01:05:00.000
And it is obviously seen as a mistake, a terrible mistake. to send the troops in. But what alternative was there? If they didn't get sent in, then the whole thing just explodes and becomes Bosnia or whatever. It's one of those things that American newspapers in the 60s used to always have this joke

136
01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:17.000
that Americans have gone holiday to Ireland and they get lost in the lanes of Ireland and they wind down the window, you'd ask a sort of farmer or something, how do I get to Dublin from here? And the farmer would say, well, to start with, I wouldn't start from here. And this is, I think, what you'd say about this story,

137
01:05:17.000 --> 01:05:33.000
that by the time when you're making that decision, do we send troops in or not, it's actually too late. The ship has sailed. You're in a massive mess, and you're not going to put it right. I think there is no scenario at this point when the fighting is so bad,

138
01:05:33.000 --> 01:05:59.000
when people are firing submachine guns and people are being shot and there's like armored cars with machine guns firing into tower blocks. There is no way you're not going to say, send troops. I mean, any British government at this point would have sent the troops. The problem, of course, is they have sent the troops and they have absolutely no political strategy about how they're going to put this right. And the time to fix it would have been 10 years previously,

139
01:05:59.000 --> 01:06:37.000
20 years previously, but not now. When you've let it get to this point. That's not very helpful advice, is it? If you're... No, it's not helpful advice. I wouldn't start from here. Advising. Yeah. So basically, they have to do it despite all the horrors that will then follow. they hadn't done it, lots of people would have been killed and everyone would have said, everyone internationally would have said, what an unbelievable act of irresponsibility of the British government, they won't, they have this army, but they won't use it to protect citizens of the United Kingdom. But at this point, it is seen by Catholics as peacekeeping. Yes, totally. I mean, this is the great irony of this, right? The British army was sent in to protect, largely to protect

140
01:06:37.000 --> 01:07:00.000
Catholics. Because I'm just thinking of Withanelle and I, famous film, set in late 69, and with Nail pretends that he's been in the army in a pub in Cumbria. Yes. He said, I've served in Northern Ireland. And the land old says, ah, crack at the Mick. Yes. But actually, that's historically inaccurate, because at this point, that is not how it is being seen. It's a very good point.

141
01:07:00.000 --> 01:07:30.000
It's seen as protecting the Catholics, exactly so. I mean, if the British troops, almost, I mean, if the politicians don't know anything about Northern Ireland, I think it's a pretty safe bet that, like, a load of 18, 19, 20, or old squatters don't. they have no idea what awaits them. There's this famous story that when they sent the first lot of reinforcements, the reinforcements were flown into an airfield and they were sort of bust into the center of Belfast or to their barracks or whatever. And they all stopped at a petrol station to buy maps of Belfast because they didn't have any maps.

142
01:07:30.000 --> 01:07:48.000
And as far as they're concerned, this is going to be pretty easy. I mean, don't forget they've been in Cyprus in the 50s. They've been in Malaya. They've been in Kenya. Malaya, they had defeated an insurgency. hadn't they? Of course they had. It's the kind of the model of how to defeat an insurgency. A lesson for the Americans of Vienna, which

143
01:07:48.000 --> 01:08:06.000
the Americans did not eat. And they think well, by these standards, this is going to be pretty straightforward. As far as I understand you know, can imagine them saying to each other. As far as I understand it, there's a load of Protestants, a lot of Catholics. The Protestants are kicking some bells out. The Catholics. Our job is to go in, tell them to stop, separate them, and then we can all go home. Bang, job done.

144
01:08:06.000 --> 01:08:39.000
And at first, they are welcomed with open arms. I mean, you can see the news footage, you can see photos, you can read the reports online. Catholic shouting at them, thank you for coming, people clapping and cheering them. Stories about in dairy, people would bring them fish and chips. You know, people would kind of winch fish and chips up the city walls to them and stuff. There was a, in Peter Taylor's book, Brits, he has a quotation from an interview with a soldier in Belfast. I felt like a knight in shining armor. Kids were following you everywhere.

145
01:08:39.000 --> 01:09:15.000
There were tea and buns and sandwiches. They'd be. you out there with trays. I must have put on about a stone, this guy says. You can see photos of soldiers leaning on their armoured cars. They're showing kids, their guns. They're chatting up housewives. It's very chummy. In fact, so chummy. The old Republicans were shocked by it. So Brendan Hughes, who was later the commander of the IRA in Belfast, said, he said, you know, I was young and I can remember people bringing them tea at the street corners. And my dad, who was an old kind of IRA bloke, and my dad objecting to him saying I never thought I would see the day

146
01:09:15.000 --> 01:09:29.000
when, you know, nationalists, Catholics would be bringing cups of tea and fish and chips and cakes and stuff to British soldiers. The end of August, so not long afterwards, Callahan flies to Northern Ireland and see how it's all going. And he goes to Derry.

147
01:09:29.000 --> 01:10:14.000
He goes to the bog side. They've already, you know, there are already no-go areas that are painted with white lines. And he steps over the white lines to go into the no-go areas. And it's fine. People, you know, thank him. They clap him and stuff. They cheer him. And when he gets back to London, the papers say, well, James Callahan, who'd been a terrible chancellor and had moved to him secretary, this Northern Ireland business has completely rebuilt his reputation. It's the man of the hour. British decisiveness and son foie have prevailed. But actually on this trip, Callaghan, because he's not an idiot, he notices that he's getting a lot of smiles from the Catholics, but he's not getting many smiles from the Unionists. And a lot of the hardliners are really not happy at all.

148
01:10:14.000 --> 01:10:41.000
As one of them said later, it felt as if we were being invaded by our own army. And he has this incredible exchange with Ian Paisley. Callaghan, who was a Baptist, said to Ian Paisley, you know, Dr. Paisley, we're all the children of God. And Tom, what does Ian Paisley say? No, we are not, Mr. Callahan. We are the children of Roth. Yeah. And I guess at some level Paisley is right.

149
01:10:41.000 --> 01:11:06.000
Because at this point, August 1969, they are the children of Roth. It is too late probably to turn back. There is too much distrust. There's too much antagonism. There's too much violence. And I think a point that's probably not brought out as often as it should be. Because in the wake of the sort of peace agreement, there's to some degree a slight culture of forgetting, which is completely understandable, by the way, politically.

150
01:11:06.000 --> 01:12:03.000
But I think what's underestimated is how much, everything that goes wrong, a lot of it is willed by people who actively wanted to see things get worse because they would suit their political interests. So people who wanted to see fighting and who connived at killing and escalation and all of this. So loyalists who wanted a showdown with the sort of the enemy within, Republicans who wanted to see the violence escalate because they wanted to do. turn people against the British. They wanted to get a united Ireland. I always think that the obvious parallel is the wars in Yugoslavia in the 1990s. So in, you know, 1994 or something in Bosnia, people would say, well, this is all the result of ancient hatreds. There's nothing anyone can do about it. Or they would say, well, actually, it's just being directed by a few malevolent, monstrous people who've led, you know, led their voters sort of down the road of chaos and hatred and all

151
01:12:03.000 --> 01:12:51.000
of this. And actually, the truth is, it's the interplay of two different things. It's the interplay of the context, the history of suspicion and conflict, which can't be wished away, but also individual decision makers. We've seen them in already, you know, Ian Paisley or whoever, or the people who organise the marches, or the people who deliberately try to provoke violence, or the people who pick up their guns, people who, for what perhaps seem to them rational, political reasons, they consciously decide to choose escalation and tension rather than, as they would call it, appeasement. And already, actually, at this point, so in late summer of 69, there are lots of people, young men largely in Belfast and Derry, who are saying, okay, well, that was

152
01:12:51.000 --> 01:13:15.000
round one, but round two is coming very soon. The state won't protect us. We need to protect ourselves. We need guns. We need to get ready. And, you know, when the shooting starts next time, we will be you know, we're not going to mess around. And the fact, they don't have long to wait. Because on the 10th of October, the British government published a report on the recent disorder. And insanely, this report was written by Sir John Hunt,

153
01:13:15.000 --> 01:13:37.000
the man who had led the expedition to conquer Everest in 1953. It wasn't the fact that he was a great mountaineer that gave him the expertise. He was also in the British Army. His recommendations were, the army should take over kind of military policing from the RUS and led us disband the be specials. The Catholics hate them. They see them as a tool of oppression, get rid. And this sets off

154
01:13:37.000 --> 01:14:28.000
three days of rioting in the Protestant Shankill Road. You have these extraordinary spectacle of mobs attacking British troops while waving the Union Jack. You know, we are the defenders of Britain against you. And the climax comes on the night of the 11th, 2,000 people marching on these high-rise flats on Divers Street, which have been guarded by lines of British troops. It's a pitch battle, stones, petrol bombs, tear gas, the usual. Somebody starts shooting in the chaos and the troops start shooting back. Sixty-six people are injured, 21 of them soldiers. Two local men, George Dickey and Herbert Howe, who were both Protestants, are shot dead by the British Army and also killed is a young RUC constable called Victor Arbuckle, who's 29. He was married with two very small

155
01:14:28.000 --> 01:15:16.000
children, a boy and a girl, and he was killed, ironically, by a sniper, not from the IRA, but from the Ulster volunteer force. He was the first Northern Irish policeman to be killed in the troubles, but he was not, alas, the last. No, far from it. So next time we will be heading into the 1970s and Northern Ireland will be spiraling towards effectively a civil war. So in the next episode, the provisional IRA will be entering the story. The British Army will be taking its first casualties. The bombings will worsen. And Ted Heath, hero of our series on 1974, enters the chat.

